          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Basement Floor Paints, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these paints for below-grade use using manufacturer labels and data sheets, especially the surfaces, moisture conditions and cure times each brand lists, along with warranty terms, odor and cleanup, and recurring themes in owner feedback. We did not conduct our own lab or wear testing on them. Where something rests on a label or on owner reports, we say so.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="below-grade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why a Basement Slab Is Different From Every Other Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most basement slabs were poured directly on gravel and soil, often without a continuous vapor barrier, especially in homes built before the 1990s. That soil holds moisture, and concrete is porous, so water vapor travels upward through the slab constantly. In summer, when the basement air is cool and the outside air is humid, condensation can add even more moisture at the surface. The slab may look dry and still be moving a surprising amount of water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paint and coatings sit on top of that vapor stream. A breathable film lets some vapor pass through; a dense film blocks it. When vapor is blocked, pressure builds underneath and pushes the coating up into blisters, or dissolves salts from the concrete that form a white, powdery layer and break the bond. That is why the same paint can last years on a garage floor at grade and peel within months in a basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Basements add two other challenges. Air movement is limited, so paints dry and cure slower and fumes linger. And many basements are cool, which slows the chemistry further. Products that list a four-hour recoat at 77 degrees may need twice as long at 60 degrees with 70 percent humidity. Planning around temperature and humidity matters as much as choosing the paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means basement floors should not be painted. It means the order of work is different: understand the moisture first, choose a product that suits the slab you actually have, and give it the conditions it needs to cure. For the general case of painting concrete above grade, our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/">best concrete floor paint</a> covers garages, patios and shops.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="read-the-slab" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Reading the Slab: What the Floor Is Telling You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before buying paint, walk the floor with a flashlight held low and look for clues. Each one tells you something about moisture and about whether the existing surface can be painted at all, and each costs nothing to check.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">White powder or crust</h3>

          <p>
            That is efflorescence: mineral salts carried to the surface by water moving through the concrete, left behind when it evaporates. A little near walls is common. Widespread efflorescence, or efflorescence that returns within weeks of cleaning, means significant water movement. Paint will not stick to it, and painting over it traps the source of the problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dark patches and tide lines</h3>

          <p>
            Darker areas that come and go with the weather, or rings that mark where water has pooled, show where moisture is reaching the surface. Patches near walls often mean water entering at the cove joint where wall meets floor. Patches in the middle of the floor can mean high groundwater or a missing vapor barrier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Peeling or bubbling old paint</h3>

          <p>
            If an old coat is lifting, flip a loose flake over. A clean underside with damp or powdery concrete beneath points to moisture. A shiny or slick underside suggests the old paint went over a sealer or never bonded. Either way, any new coat has to go over sound concrete, not over the failing layer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The plastic sheet test</h3>

          <p>
            Then run the test that settles the question. Tape 18 by 18 inch squares of clear plastic to the floor in three or four places, sealing every edge, and leave them at least 16 hours, ideally a full day or two. Condensation under the plastic or darkened concrete means too much moisture for most paints. A dry result means you can proceed, though breathable products remain the safer bet below grade.
          </p>

