        <div id="fix-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Fix Water Problems Before You Open a Can
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint is a finish, not a water-management system. If the slab tests damp, the cheapest and most effective fixes are almost always outside the house. Make sure gutters are clear, downspouts discharge at least 4 to 6 feet from the foundation, and the soil around the house slopes away from the walls. Those three changes alone resolve a large share of damp basements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inside, control humidity. A basement held around 50 percent relative humidity has far less surface condensation and is more comfortable for paint and people. A properly sized dehumidifier is often the single most useful purchase before painting; our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best dehumidifier for basements</a> covers sizing and drain options. Run it for at least a week before the moisture test and throughout the cure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seal obvious entry points. Cracks in the floor and the joint where floor meets wall can be sealed with products designed for concrete, and damp walls can be treated with masonry waterproofers. Our guides to the <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">best basement wall sealer</a> and <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">fixing a damp basement</a> go into detail. Retest the floor after those fixes; a slab that failed before may pass.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HARD STOP</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Standing water means no paint, period.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If water pools on the floor after storms, seeps through cracks, or the plastic test shows droplets even after fixing gutters and humidity, the basement needs waterproofing, drainage or a sump system before any coating. Paint over an active leak fails, and it hides the warning signs while it does.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Keep an eye on mold too. Damp basements often have musty odors or visible growth on walls, stored boxes or wood framing. Paint does not fix mold, and coating over a moldy surface can trap the problem. Clean affected areas with an appropriate product, as covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-mold-remover/">best mold remover</a>, and resolve the moisture that feeds it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="product-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Paint, Sealer or Epoxy: Choosing by Slab Condition
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right product depends on what the moisture test showed, not on which product looks best in a photo. Basement floor products fall into three broad groups, and each one suits a different slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Breathable latex and acrylic floor paint.</strong> Products such as DRYLOK Latex Concrete Floor Paint and Valspar Porch, Floor and Patio enamel allow some vapor through. They are the most forgiving choice for basements that pass a moisture test but sit on soil that is never fully dry. They are softer than epoxy, but they fail gracefully, wearing thin rather than peeling in sheets.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Epoxy-modified paint and two-part epoxy.</strong> These form harder, denser films that resist stains and scuffs better but tolerate vapor less. They belong on basement slabs that test clearly dry, ideally ones with a vapor barrier under the concrete. The Rust-Oleum basement kit and the BEHR and KILZ one-part epoxy paints fall here. For a deeper look at two-part options, see our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-floor-coating/">best concrete floor coatings</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Penetrating sealers.</strong> Silane, siloxane and silicate sealers soak into the concrete without forming a film, so there is nothing to peel. They are the best choice for slabs with persistent minor dampness, or for anyone who wants a protected floor that will later be covered with rugs or floating flooring. They do not add color. If a borderline slab is the reality, a sealer now and paint later, after moisture fixes have proven themselves, is a reasonable path.
          </p>

