        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Basement Prep: Cleaning, Etching and Drying
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear the floor completely and remove old carpet pads, tack strips and adhesive. Scrape off drywall mud, paint drips and anything else stuck to the slab. Loose or failing old paint must come off, using a scraper, a floor stripper or a rented grinder with a vacuum attachment. Mark any cracks for repair later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean efflorescence and dirt next. Dry-brush efflorescence with a stiff brush, then treat it with an efflorescence cleaner or diluted acid as the product directs, and rinse thoroughly. Degrease oil and laundry spills with a concrete degreaser. Basements also accumulate a fine film of dust and residue that needs a thorough scrub; a mop alone is not enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Etch smooth concrete so the paint can grip. A citric acid etch is milder and easier in an enclosed space than muriatic acid. Wet the floor, apply the etch, scrub it in, and rinse at least twice, using a wet vacuum to pull up the rinse water since basements rarely have floor drains positioned where you need them. Patch cracks with a paintable concrete repair product after etching.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then let the slab dry, which takes longer below grade than anywhere else. Run a dehumidifier and fans, and allow at least two or three days after wet cleaning before painting. Recheck with a plastic sheet if you are unsure. Paint applied to a slab that is still holding rinse water is one of the most common causes of early basement paint failure.
          </p>

