        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which paint works best on a basement slab?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A breathable acrylic or latex floor paint is the safest choice for most basement floors, because it tolerates the small amount of vapor that moves through below-grade concrete. On slabs that test clearly dry, an epoxy-modified paint or a water-based two-part basement epoxy gives a tougher finish. Avoid ordinary wall paint, which is too soft for floors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint a basement floor that gets damp?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if it gets damp from below. Paint and coatings on a slab with active moisture blister and peel. Fix exterior drainage, control humidity with a dehumidifier, and seal cracks first, then retest. If the slab still shows moisture, use a penetrating sealer rather than paint, or address the water with waterproofing before applying any film coating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does basement floor paint stop water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Floor paint is a finish, not a waterproofing system. Breathable paints allow some vapor through by design, and even dense coatings will be pushed off by water pressure from below. To stop water, fix gutters, grading and drainage, seal cracks, and consider interior drainage or a sump system. Paint goes on afterward, once the slab stays dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my basement floor is too wet to paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tape an 18 by 18 inch piece of clear plastic to the floor, seal the edges, and leave it at least 16 hours. If droplets form or the concrete underneath darkens, there is too much moisture. Check several spots, including near walls. Widespread efflorescence or musty odors are other signs the slab needs attention first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use epoxy or paint on a basement floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use epoxy on a basement slab that tests clearly dry and needs a tough, stain-resistant surface, such as a workshop or laundry area. Use breathable floor paint on older slabs or ones that pass the moisture test only marginally. Paint is cheaper and more forgiving; epoxy is harder but less tolerant of moisture from below.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are typical dry and cure times below grade?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most basement floor paints are dry to the touch in a few hours and ready for a second coat in four to twelve hours, but cool, humid basements stretch those times. Allow a day before walking on it in socks, several days before light furniture, and a week or more before heavy appliances or rugs. A dehumidifier speeds curing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to prime a basement floor before painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many basement floor paints are self-priming on bare concrete, often with a thinned or light first coat, but check the label. Use the manufacturer's recommended primer over very porous, chalky or previously sealed surfaces, or when changing from oil-based to water-based paint. A bonding primer can also help over sound old paint that has been scuff-sanded.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
