            For a dry basement slab that needs a tough finish, Rust-Oleum's water-based basement epoxy kit is our first choice, with BEHR's one-part epoxy paint as the tintable, easier alternative and KILZ as the budget option. For older or borderline slabs, a breathable latex like DRYLOK floor paint or Valspar's porch and floor enamel is the safer bet, and Sure Step belongs on every set of concrete basement stairs.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The product matters less than the order of operations. Read the slab, fix the water, test again, clean and etch, dry it thoroughly, and paint in warm, ventilated conditions with a dehumidifier running. Follow that sequence and a basement floor will stay painted for years; skip it and even the best product in the store will lift.
          </p>
